  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\n- Chance for Showers and Thunderstorms Today\n\nThe bottom line is that most thunderstorms that develop today will\nnot be severe, but will need to watch the 18z-22z window east of\nUS 131 for some scattered thunderstorm wind gusts of 40-60 mph\n(isolated 60+ mph), mostly sub-severe hail, and perhaps enough low\nlevel shear / SRH to stretch and tilt some horizontal vorticity to\nget some mesocyclonic development. That latter risk, however,\nseems to be unlikely at this point though a short-lived spin up\ntornado can't be ruled out east of US 131 this afternoon/evening.\nWe feel that most locations should get through the day without\nstorm damage, but would not be surprised if some scattered pockets\nof tree damage and power outages do develop.\n\nBeyond 22z and through about 03z or so, an isolated shower or\nstorm may redevelop over central Lake Michigan into central Lower\nMI and move SE with time, though these are unlikely to be strong\nas peak daytime heating will be in the rearview mirror. \n\n- Warmer Next Week, Occasionally Humid and Stormy\n\nQuiet days are anticipated from Thursday (aside from a rogue pop\nup daytime shower) through at least Saturday before changes begin\nto take shape into next week. First, the warmth. Ensemble \nguidance supports above normal temperatures next week with a \nsubset of \"grand ensemble\" (ECE/GEFS/CMC) membership supporting \nhighs in the 90s at times. In fact, roughly 1/3 of all members\nindicate highs getting into the 90s early next week. Ensemble mean\n500 mb heights keep climbing a bit higher and now it appears even\n590 dm could be in play for southern Lower MI. However, any MCS\ninfluence via a ridge rider pattern could alter the ridge's\nnorthern extent and put us in a subsequently stormier and\nsomewhat cooler pattern. Currently, ensemble mean 850mb temps are\nquite high for early September with 20C+ being shown especially\nearly next week. With outdoor school activities getting into full\nswing, there may be some uncomfortable periods of heat/humidity to\ndeal with depending on how this pans out.\n\nSecond, occasionally humid air and associated MCSs / warm frontal\nstorm development are likely to occur across the Midwest and \npotentially Great Lakes as well. PWAT values look anomalous for \nearly September with waves of 2.00\"+ looking plausible into next \nweek. Deterministic guidance agrees that LLJ development of 40 kts\nor greater will occur somewhere in the Midwest starting as early \nas Sunday. How far this angles into Lower MI is unclear at this \npoint, but we'll be close enough to have some periodic threats for\nboth nocturnal and daytime thunderstorms. Too early for \nspecifics, and part of the time we may be capped, but pattern \nrecognition would say we should have periodic rain chances. NBM \nPOPs are not high at this point (20-30%) but those may go up with \ntime.",
